---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---
<DocsLayout title="Worktree Management — Band Docs">
  <h1>Worktree Management</h1>
  <p>
    Worktrees in Band are <strong>git worktree</strong>-based isolated environments for each
    branch you work on. Every worktree gets its own directory on disk with a full copy of the
    repository at the specified branch, allowing you to work on multiple branches simultaneously
    without switching contexts.
  </p>

  <h2>Creating Worktrees</h2>
  <p>
    You can create a new worktree from the dashboard or the CLI. Each worktree corresponds
    to a git worktree branch.
  </p>

  <h3>From the Dashboard</h3>
  <p>
    Click the <strong>+</strong> button next to a repo name. A dialog appears with three fields:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Branch name</strong> &mdash; The name of the new branch (e.g., <code>feature/auth</code>).</li>
    <li><strong>Base branch</strong> (optional) &mdash; The branch to base the new worktree on. Defaults to the repo's default branch.</li>
    <li><strong>Initial prompt</strong> (optional) &mdash; A task prompt for the coding agent. If provided, the agent starts working immediately after worktree creation.</li>
  </ul>

  <figure class="my-8 mx-auto max-w-md overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-border shadow-lg">
    <img
      src="/screenshot-add-worktree.png"
      alt="The Add Worktree dialog in the Band dashboard, with fields for branch name, base branch, and an optional initial prompt."
      width="430"
      height="426"
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
      class="block h-auto w-full"
    />
    <figcaption class="border-t border-border bg-card/50 px-4 py-2 text-center text-sm text-muted-foreground">
      The Add Worktree dialog &mdash; branch name, base branch, and an optional initial prompt.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>From the CLI</h3>
  <pre><code># Create worktree and start an agent task immediately
band worktrees create my-app feat/login --prompt "Implement login page"

# Create worktree without starting a task
band worktrees create my-app feat/experiment

# Create from a specific base branch
band worktrees create my-app feat/api --base develop</code></pre>
  <p>
    Worktree creation is <strong>idempotent</strong> &mdash; creating an existing worktree
    simply returns its path without duplicating anything.
  </p>

  <h3>Untracked file copying</h3>
  <p>
    A fresh worktree only contains files git knows about. Untracked files like
    <code>.env</code>, local credential overrides, or IDE settings are absent by
    default. Configure them to be copied from the repo's main checkout into
    every new worktree via either of two declarative sources at the repo root:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>.band/config.json::workspace.copyFiles</code> &mdash; explicit list of paths
      (literals or globs). See <a href="/docs/configuration">Repo Configuration</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>.worktreeinclude</code> &mdash; gitignore-syntax patterns. Only files
      that match a pattern AND are gitignored are copied (Claude Code parity).
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    When both sources are present, the union is copied. Copies happen after
    <code>git worktree add</code> and before the <code>setup</code> script runs,
    so the setup script can read <code>.env</code> just like it can in the main
    checkout. Missing source files are skipped with a warning.
  </p>

  <h2>Worktree List</h2>
  <p>
    Worktrees appear nested under their parent repo in the dashboard. Each worktree card
    displays:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Branch name</strong> &mdash; The git branch this worktree tracks.</li>
    <li><strong>Agent status indicator</strong> &mdash; A colored dot showing whether the coding agent is working or needs attention. See <a href="/docs/agent-status">Agent Status</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Setup status</strong> &mdash; Shows whether the setup script is running, completed, or failed.</li>
    <li><strong>Git status</strong> &mdash; Indicates dirty files, unpushed commits, and sync state (ahead, behind, diverged).</li>
    <li><strong>CI status</strong> &mdash; Shows the current CI pipeline state (pending, running, success, failure, cancelled, merged).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>CI Status</h2>
  <p>
    Each worktree card shows a small icon next to the branch name reflecting
    the current GitHub Actions state for that branch. Hovering reveals a
    tooltip describing the state ("CI passed", "CI failed", and so on); clicking
    the icon opens the relevant pull request, workflow run, or check page on
    GitHub.
  </p>

  <figure class="my-8 mx-auto max-w-sm overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-border shadow-lg">
    <img
      src="/screenshot-ci-passed.png"
      alt="A worktree row in the Band dashboard showing a green CI status icon next to the branch name, with a 'CI passed' tooltip displayed."
      width="272"
      height="83"
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
      class="block h-auto w-full"
    />
    <figcaption class="border-t border-border bg-card/50 px-4 py-2 text-center text-sm text-muted-foreground">
      Hover the CI icon to see the current state &mdash; click it to open the workflow run on GitHub.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>How CI status is resolved</h3>
  <p>
    Band reads CI state from <strong>GitHub Actions</strong> by calling
    GitHub's GraphQL API through the <code>gh</code> CLI. For each registered
    worktree branch it asks GitHub for:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>The most recent open or merged pull request on that branch.</li>
    <li>The check suites and workflow runs attached to the head commit.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If a branch has multiple workflows running, Band aggregates them and shows
    the most significant state &mdash; <em>failure</em> beats <em>running</em>
    beats <em>pending</em> beats <em>cancelled</em> beats <em>success</em>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Only GitHub Actions workflows are surfaced. Third-party check providers
    (CircleCI, Buildkite, and so on) that report through the GitHub Checks API
    are filtered out.
  </p>

  <h3>Authentication</h3>
  <p>
    Band uses whatever credentials your <code>gh</code> CLI is configured with
    &mdash; there is no separate token to configure inside Band. To enable CI
    status, run:
  </p>
  <pre><code>gh auth login</code></pre>
  <p>
    Once <code>gh auth status</code> reports a successful login, Band starts
    resolving CI state for branches in repositories you have access to. GitHub
    Enterprise hosts work too &mdash; authenticate the matching hostname with
    <code>gh auth login --hostname &lt;your-enterprise-host&gt;</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    If <code>gh</code> is not installed or not authenticated, Band leaves the
    CI column empty rather than throwing an error.
  </p>

  <h3>States</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pending</strong> &mdash; A workflow is queued but has not started yet.</li>
    <li><strong>Running</strong> &mdash; A workflow is currently executing for the head commit.</li>
    <li><strong>Success</strong> &mdash; All workflows for the head commit passed.</li>
    <li><strong>Failure</strong> &mdash; At least one workflow failed.</li>
    <li><strong>Cancelled</strong> &mdash; The most recent workflow run was cancelled.</li>
    <li><strong>Merged</strong> &mdash; The branch's pull request has been merged. Not shown on the repo's default branch.</li>
    <li><strong>None</strong> &mdash; No workflows are configured, no commits have been pushed yet, or <code>gh</code> is unavailable.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Opening a Worktree</h2>
  <p>
    Clicking a worktree opens it inside the dashboard. The active worktree is highlighted
    with a left border accent.
  </p>

  <h2>Removing Worktrees</h2>
  <p>
    You can remove a worktree from the context menu (right-click) or via the CLI:
  </p>
  <pre><code>band worktrees remove my-app feat/login</code></pre>
  <p>
    Before removal, Band runs the repo's <code>teardown</code> script if one is defined in
    <code>.band/config.json</code>. The teardown script is non-fatal &mdash; removal proceeds
    even if it fails.
  </p>
  <p>
    If the worktree has unmerged pull requests, dirty files, or unpushed commits, a confirmation
    dialog appears warning you about potential data loss. You must explicitly confirm deletion in
    these cases.
  </p>

  <h2>Worktree Context Menu</h2>
  <p>
    Right-clicking a worktree reveals additional actions:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Copy path</strong> &mdash; Copy the worktree path to clipboard.</li>
    <li><strong>Open in Finder</strong> &mdash; Reveal the worktree directory.</li>
    <li><strong>Run setup</strong> &mdash; Manually re-run the setup script (only shown if a setup script is defined).</li>
    <li><strong>Run teardown</strong> &mdash; Manually run the teardown script.</li>
    <li><strong>Git pull</strong> &mdash; Pull the latest changes for this branch.</li>
    <li><strong>Git push</strong> &mdash; Push local commits to the remote.</li>
    <li><strong>Delete worktree</strong> &mdash; Remove the worktree and its worktree (not available for the default branch).</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    To review and commit pending changes inside a worktree, open the
    <a href="/docs/changes-view">Changes view</a> &mdash; it's the diff
    reviewer where per-file revert and the commit dialog live.
  </p>

  <h2>Naming Conventions</h2>
  <p>
    Worktree names are derived from their branch names. Band supports standard git branch
    naming conventions including slashes for namespacing (e.g., <code>feature/auth</code>,
    <code>fix/bug-123</code>). Branch names must not contain control characters or path
    traversals (<code>../</code>).
  </p>
  <p>
    Worktree directories are stored in the configured worktrees folder (default:
    <code>~/.band/worktrees</code>), organized by repo name and branch.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
